# New demo project — agent scaffolding procedure

Use this rule when the user asks to create a new prototype or demo project.
Follow the steps below **in order**. Do not skip ahead to code generation
until the questions are answered.

---

## Step 1 — Ask for the project name

Ask the user for a **kebab-case slug** for the new demo. Explain the rules:

- Lowercase letters and hyphens only (e.g. `team-planner`, `schedule-view`).
- This becomes the folder name (`demos/<slug>/`), the URL path (`/<slug>/`), and
  the button label on the Projects hub.
- Keep it short and descriptive.

If the user gives you a name that doesn't match the rules (e.g. spaces, capitals),
suggest a corrected version and confirm before proceeding.

If a folder `demos/<slug>/` already exists, tell the user and ask whether to
proceed (which will overwrite scaffold files) or choose a different name.

---

## Step 2 — Scaffold the workspace

Once the name is confirmed, create the full demo workspace following the exact
layout and config described in `.cursor/rules/demo-projects.mdc`.

Key files to generate:

- `demos/<slug>/package.json` — `name` must match the folder slug exactly; include
  `"lofi-kit": "*"`, `react`, `react-dom`, and the standard Vite scripts
  (`dev`, `build`, `preview`, `test`, `test:watch`).
  **IMPORTANT:** use `"lofi-kit": "*"` — NOT `"workspace:*"`. The `workspace:`
  protocol is pnpm/yarn syntax and is NOT supported by npm. Using `"*"` is the
  correct npm-workspaces-native way to reference a sibling workspace package.
  Resolution at build time happens via vite's `resolve.alias` and tsconfig `paths`
  anyway — the npm dependency entry is declarative only.
- `demos/<slug>/vite.config.ts` — Mirror `demos/bracket-demo/vite.config.ts` exactly,
  including `LOFI_VIA_HUB` / `LOFI_HUB_PORT` env handling, `base` switching,
  `server.hmr.clientPort`, SCSS `loadPaths`, and the `lofi-kit` resolve alias.
- `demos/<slug>/tsconfig.json` — Match the bracket-demo tsconfig; set
  `tsBuildInfoFile` to `../../node_modules/.tmp/<slug>.tsbuildinfo`.
- `demos/<slug>/index.html` — Standard Vite entry; `<title>` set to the display name.
- `demos/<slug>/src/main.tsx` — React 19 root (`createRoot`).
- `demos/<slug>/src/App.tsx` — Minimal shell (see below).

Also register the new demo in root config files:

- Add `{ "path": "./demos/<slug>/tsconfig.json" }` to the `references` array in
  `tsconfig.json`.
- Add `"demos/<slug>/vite.config.ts"` to the `include` array in `tsconfig.node.json`.

Do NOT run `npm install` from the agent. Instruct the user to run it once
after scaffolding is complete — **always from the repo root**, not from inside the
demo folder. Running `npm install` from within `demos/<slug>/` will fail because
npm cannot resolve workspace-relative paths without the root workspace context.

---

## Step 3 — Generate the first screen (Lo-fi by default)

**Fidelity is always Lo-fi.** Never ask the user whether they want lo-fi or hi-fi.
Never use colors, custom fonts, or external UI libraries. Everything is built from
`lofi-kit` components and the existing SCSS token system.

### Before writing any screen code

Read these files:

1. `docs/PATTERNS.md` — component catalog, visual rules, composition patterns
2. `docs/UX_PATTERNS.md` — interaction rules, P1–P10 pattern inventory
3. `LOFI_BLOCKS.md` — quick-lookup table, "Forbidden raw HTML" substitutions

### Reference implementation

Use `demos/bracket-demo/src/components/TeamManagement/` as the **gold standard**
for how a well-built lo-fi screen looks:

- Every component imported from `lofi-kit` (no raw `<button>`, `<select>`,
  `<input>`, or `<table>`).
- `LOFIText` for all copy — no raw `<span>`, `<p>`, `<h1>` in component JSX.
- `LOFIButton` for every action.
- `LOFIField` + `LOFIInput` / `LOFISelect` / `LOFICheckbox` for forms.
- `LOFIModal` for overlays; `LOFIPanel` for side panels.
- `LOFITable` (via `@tanstack/react-table`) for data tables.
- BEM class names with SCSS tokens — no inline `style={{}}`.

### `App.tsx` starting point

```tsx
import { LOFIText } from 'lofi-kit';

export function App() {
  return (
    <main style={{ padding: '2rem' }}>
      <LOFIText variant="caps">Your Demo Title</LOFIText>
    </main>
  );
}
```

Build the first screen from the user's description, following
`lofi-prototyping.mdc` — plan first (component map, states, mocked data),
then execute. Do not start generating screen code until the plan is confirmed.

---

## Step 4 — Post-scaffold instructions for the user

After scaffolding, tell the user to run the following commands **from the repo root**:

```bash
# Always from the repo root — NOT from inside demos/<slug>/
npm install
npm run dev
```

The new demo appears automatically on the Projects hub at `http://127.0.0.1:5172/`.
No CI or config changes needed — the hub discovers `demos/*` automatically.

**If `npm run dev` fails with `EADDRINUSE` (port already in use)**, a previous dev
session is still running. Kill it first, then restart:

```bash
pkill -f "dev-gateway.mjs"; pkill -f "vite"; sleep 1 && npm run dev
```

**To run only the new demo** (no hub, no Storybook, avoids port conflicts):

```bash
npm run dev -w <slug>
# Demo available at http://127.0.0.1:5174/ (or next available port)
```

To build:

```bash
npm run build -w <slug>       # check it compiles
npm run demo-build            # full Pages-shaped output in public/
```

---

## What this rule does NOT do

- Generate hi-fi or themed prototypes (future workflow).
- Add a monorepo-level React Router or shell app.
- Create test files automatically (add tests separately when coverage is requested).
